  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Cap-d'Ail, a small town squeezed between Monaco and Nice, reserves the eastern part of its Pointe des Douaniers beach for dogs. A pebble setting typical of this stretch of the Riviera, with views over the sea and the surrounding rocks.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>It's the only option in the town — handy if you're based in Monaco or nearby and looking for the closest dog-friendly spot to the Principality, which itself allows no beach for dogs.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Sentier des Douaniers coastal path, which follows the coast from Cap-d'Ail, is also a nice walk before or after the swim.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Golfe Bleu beach in Roquebrune-Cap-Martin and the Menton cani-plage are less than 20 minutes away by car, to vary the spots in this area.</p>
